---
There is a field behind this text. It is a grid of characters, one per cell, redrawn
thirty times a second, and it is not a picture of anything — it is a word being pulled
apart by a noise function and put back together again.

The idea is stolen wholesale from Andreas Gysin. What follows is what I found when I took
his front page apart, and what I did differently.

:::
<p class="stage">
  <a href="https://github.com/ertdfgcvb/play.core">github.com/ertdfgcvb/play.core</a> ·
  <a href="https://ertdfgcvb.xyz">ertdfgcvb.xyz</a> ·
  <a href="https://play.ertdfgcvb.xyz">the playground</a>
</p>
:::

## the shape of the thing

A *program* fills a grid. A *renderer* writes the grid to the DOM. That is the entire
architecture, and it is better than it sounds, because it means the program never touches
the document and the renderer never knows what it is drawing.

    boot(ctx, g)      once, after the first measurement
    frame(ctx, g)     the whole grid at once. the fast path.
    main(x,y,i,ctx)   one cell, returns a glyph index. the readable path.

A cell is not a character. It is an *index* into a ramp — `' .:-=+*#%@'` — so brightness
and glyph are the same number, and `bgchars` can swap the alphabet under a running program
without the program being told.

## what the deobfuscation showed

The front page ships as one 18KB minified module. Beaten back into shape, it turns out the
renderer in production is byte-for-byte the algorithm in the public repo. There is no
secret. The speed is in five specific decisions:

**Two colours.** Every cell picks one of two, by a single threshold. A row of two hundred
cells emits three or four `<span>`s rather than two hundred, because the renderer opens a
new one only when the style actually changes.

**A style equal to the container's is not emitted at all.** `cell.color === settings.color`
becomes `null`, so long stretches are bare text inside one tag.

**Only changed rows are written.** Each row is compared against a copy of the last frame,
and `innerHTML` is assigned only where something moved.

**A glyph→index map.** The letter animation asks "which index is this character" once per
glyph per frame; `indexOf` over a hundred-glyph charset would put a linear scan on the hot
path. One object literal removes it.

**The overlay runs every other frame.** `frame % 2`. Nobody can see the difference.

> The thing I expected to find and did not: any attempt to avoid allocation. The runner
> does `buffer[idx] = {...buffer[idx], ...out}` for every cell of every frame, and the
> renderer does `backBuffer[i] = {...cell}` for every changed cell. Fullscreen, that is
> somewhere north of twenty thousand short-lived objects per frame. It runs at sixty
> anyway, which says more about how good the collector has got than about the code.

## what is different here

A cell here is two integers in two typed arrays — `Uint16Array` for the glyph, `Uint8Array`
for the style — sized once and mutated in place. After boot, a frame allocates nothing.

Beyond that, four changes:

**A monochrome fast path.** If no cell carries a style and no cell carries a link — the
common case, and the case every backdrop is in — the frame is built as one string and
assigned to `textContent` once. No spans, no parsing, one DOM write for the whole screen.

**The rectangle is cached.** `getBoundingClientRect()` forces layout. play.core calls it
inside the frame loop. Here it is read on resize and not again.

**Glyphs are escaped once, at load.** The original writes `html += cell.char` straight into
markup. Its own ramps are safe by construction, but this one can be handed a ramp from a
block header, and `bgchars` is allowed to contain `<`, `&` and `>` — they are glyphs, and
they are drawn as glyphs. A parallel array of entity-escaped strings is built when the ramp
is loaded, and the markup path indexes that one.

**Two layers, not one.** The field is monochrome and stays on the fast path; the floating
links live on a second `<pre>` above it, where the slow path is affordable because almost
every row of it is empty and therefore skipped.

## the floating part

The links drifting behind this page are not new markup. They are the page's own navigation
and backlinks, harvested from the DOM and composited into a character grid — which is what
Gysin's `post()` does with his `<main>`, and it is the right call: nothing floats back there
that is not already reachable, so both layers are `aria-hidden` and nothing is duplicated
for a screen reader or for `lynx`.

They arrive by split-flap. A character does not appear; it counts up through the charset
until it reaches the one it wants. Because the charset by then holds the ramp *and* every
letter the links need, the words assemble out of the same texture the field is drawn from.

## the cost

Measured on this machine, at a hundred and sixty by fifty cells, steady state:

    tenprint  0.05 ms      warp    0.98 ms      moire   2.5 ms
    subdiv    0.09 ms      plasma  0.92 ms      rd      2.0 ms
    rain      0.20 ms      strata  1.4 ms       sdf     8   ms
    life      0.31 ms      spiral  1.1 ms

At thirty frames a second, one millisecond of work is three per cent of one core. The
budget is not the arithmetic; it is the DOM, and the monochrome path is there to keep the
DOM out of it.

Everything pauses off-screen and on a hidden tab, and under `prefers-reduced-motion` the
field is composed exactly once and then left alone. A still noise field is a texture. A
moving one, behind text, is a hazard.

See [[ascii]] for all of them running at once, [[background]] to change the one behind this
page, and [[abstract]] for the half that are not pictures of anything.
